WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A secure token handling baseline for .NET starts with purpose.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:15.800
Know whether a token is an access token, ID token, refresh token, account workflow token, antiforgery token, or Data Protection-protected payload.

3
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.820
Each type has a different trust boundary and validation path.

4
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.820
Validate JWT bearer tokens fully.

5
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:35.880
Use maintained authentication components, confirm issuer, audience, lifetime, signature, signing key, and expected algorithm behavior, and interpret claims only after validation succeeds.

6
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.420
A readable token is not automatically a trusted token.

7
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.340
Protect tokens in storage and transport.

8
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.080
Keep tokens out of URLs, logs, frontend assets, public artifacts, and broad support tooling.

9
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:56.860
Use protected channels, review cookie behavior, and choose client storage patterns according to the client type and sensitivity of the token.

10
00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.540
Separate authentication from authorization.

11
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:06.600
Claims, scopes, audiences, tenants, and resource identifiers need server-side interpretation.

12
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:15.820
A valid token establishes context, but endpoint policy, resource checks, tenant boundaries, and business rules decide what is allowed.

13
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:27.220
Finally, manage refresh tokens, lifetimes, revocation, signing keys, Data Protection keys, redaction, monitoring, and compromise response deliberately.

14
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:35.960
Secure token handling is a lifecycle discipline that connects identity design, application code, infrastructure, and operations.
